  "sourceSolutions": [
    {
      "source": "1062",
      "task": "Prove the general decimal concatenation rule for squaring a two-digit number ending in5.",
      "solution": "The tens digit n is an integer1 through9. (10n+5)^2=100n^2+100n+25=100n(n+1)+25. Multiplication by100 shifts the integer n(n+1) two decimal places; adding25 places exactly the suffix25. This proves the rule for every allowed tens digit, not just selected numerical examples.",
      "domain": "Two-digit positive decimal integer; n=1,...,9."
    },
    {
      "source": "1063",
      "task": "Find the two-digit number whose square is written with the listed digits0,2,3,5; preserve any ambiguity about whether every listed digit must occur.",
      "sourceQualification": "The prose lists four digits but does not separately specify digit multiplicity or whether omission is allowed. Requiring every listed digit to occur is the conventional puzzle reading; reading only a permitted digit alphabet with repetitions and omissions gives a different complete set. The original intended reading is retained as UNKNOWN rather than silently adding a rule.",
      "conventionalAllListedDigitsSolution": "If all four listed digits occur and no other digits occur, a two-digit positive integer square has at most four digits and hence each listed digit must occur exactly once. A square ending in0 would end in00, contradicting the single zero. It therefore ends in5, with a root ending in5 and a square ending in25. The remaining prefix digits are0 and3; the only standard four-digit possibility is3025, not a leading-zero spelling. Thus the root is55, since55^2=3025.",
      "permittedAlphabetWithRepetitionAndOmissionSolution": "A square's last digit among0,2,3,5 can only be0 or5. Its root must end in0 or5. For roots10,20,...,90, only50^2=2500 uses permitted digits. For roots15,25,...,95 the squares are225,625,1225,2025,3025,4225,5625,7225,9025; precisely15,45,55 qualify. The complete broader set is{15,45,50,55}.",
      "conditionalSourceResults": "All four actually occur: {55}. Only a permitted alphabet, repetitions/omissions allowed: {15,45,50,55}. These are qualified results, not recovery of the original intended convention."
    },
    {
      "source": "1064",
      "task": "Find all two-digit numbers for which the reversed number is also two-digit and their sum is a natural-number square.",
      "solution": "Write10a+b and10b+a with integers1<=a,b<=9. Their sum is11(a+b), between22 and198. If it equals k^2, primality of11 gives11|k. The positive square root lies between5 and14, so k=11 and a+b=11. With digit bounds, a=2,...,9 and b=11-a. The complete ordered-number set is{29,38,47,56,65,74,83,92}; a reversed pair contributes two different numbers, not one unordered representative.",
      "domain": "Both digits nonzero and at most9; neither a zero-leading reversal nor a three-digit number is admitted."
    },
    {
      "source": "1065",
      "task": "Prove that the product of any four consecutive natural numbers plus1 is a natural-number square.",
      "solution": "Let the four numbers be n,n+1,n+2,n+3. Put t=n(n+3)=n^2+3n. Then(n+1)(n+2)=t+2, and n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)+1=t(t+2)+1=(t+1)^2=(n^2+3n+1)^2. For either convention about0 in the natural numbers, n>=0 and t+1>=1 is a positive integer; no choice of the zero-membership convention is needed.",
      "sourceConventionResolved": false,
      "optionalBoundary": "If n=0 is permitted, the expression is1, still the square of the positive natural number1."
    },
    {
      "source": "1066",
      "task": "Prove impossibility for every60-digit arrangement having exactly30 zeros and30 ones.",
      "solution": "Every such positive decimal integer has digit sum30. It is divisible by3 but not by9. If it were m^2, the prime3 dividing m^2 would divide m, so9 would divide m^2, a contradiction. Equivalent residue check: the number is3 modulo9, while square residues modulo9 are0,1,4,7. The argument does not assume which digit is last or how the zeros/ones are ordered.",
      "domain": "Exactly60 digits in ordinary decimal notation; leading digit is necessarily1. Last digit may be0 or1."
    },
    {
      "source": "1067",
      "task": "Determine the last decimal digit of43^43-17^17.",
      "solution": "Powers of a number ending in3 have unit-digit cycle3,9,7,1;43 is3 modulo4, giving7. Powers ending in7 have cycle7,9,3,1;17 is1 modulo4, also giving7. The difference is positive and0 modulo10, so its last digit is0."
    },
    {
      "source": "1068",
      "task": "Find every two-digit decimal integer satisfying twice its digit sum equals its digit product, without inferring uniqueness from the singular wording.",
      "solution": "Write tens digit a=1,...,9 and units digit b=0,...,9. The equation is2(a+b)=ab, or(a-2)(b-2)=4. Neither digit can be0,1 or2 in a valid solution, so the factors are positive. The positive divisor pairs of4 are(1,4),(2,2),(4,1), giving digit pairs(3,6),(4,4),(6,3). The complete number set is{36,44,63}. Checks:2(3+6)=18=3*6;2(4+4)=16=4*4;2(6+3)=18=6*3.",
      "wordingQualification": "The original singular request does not make the conditions uniquely solvable. Do not silently assume equal or distinct digits."
    },
    {
      "source": "1069",
      "task": "Decide whether an integer-numerator fraction with denominator20 lies strictly between4/13 and5/13.",
      "solution": "For p/20 the strict bounds are80<13p<100 after multiplication by260>0. The integer p=7 satisfies80<91<100. It is the only integer between80/13 and100/13, so7/20 is a valid witness. At p=6,78<80; at p=8,104>100. The source asks existence, so the witness and its two strict comparisons settle the requested task; uniqueness is an additional computed property, not an extra original command.",
      "endpoints": "Both original endpoints are open; no endpoint is inserted."
    }
